<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Hocabá</h1><p class="st-meta">1441 CE – 1542 CE (approx.) · capital Hocabá</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Maya province of north-central Yucatán, one of the territories that emerged after the fall of Mayapan in the 1440s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hocabá was one of the provinces (kuchkabalob) formed in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán</a> after the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan</a> collapsed around 1441. It occupied a compact area south-east of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merida-yucatan">Mérida</a>, between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ceh-pech">Ceh Pech</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ah-kin-chel">Ah Kin Chel</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sotuta">Sotuta</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The province was governed from the town of Hocabá by a ruler of the Iuit lineage, who held authority over the batabs, the heads of individual towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homun">Homún</a>. Like its neighbours, it lived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> farming, beekeeping and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> weaving. It is known mainly from Spanish colonial records studied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-roys">Ralph Roys</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hocabá submitted to the Spanish in the early 1540s and was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-yucatan">Province of Yucatán</a>. Its towns became colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1441 CE – 1542 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.38%;width:2.01%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1441 CE – 1542 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hocabá</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hocaba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=hocaba" aria-label="Hocabá on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2279 1408 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hocabá lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2599 1664L2591 1670M2599 1664L4910 989M2599 1664L2590 1658"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2591 1670h0M4910 989h0M2590 1658h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2599 1664h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2599 1664h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hocabá. <a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=hocaba">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
